<article class="env-variant" id="0705">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0705">Variant <span data-tag="0705">9.7.2.13</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be an uncountable regular cardinal and let $K$ be a $\kappa $-small simplicial set. Then $K$ is sifted if and only if it is nonempty and the cartesian product functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ T: \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } \times \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } \quad \quad (X,Y) \mapsto X \times Y \]
</div>
<p> preserves $K$-indexed colimits. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $\kappa $-small colimits are universal in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ (Proposition <a data-tag="05WA" href="/tag/05WA">7.7.6.7</a>), the functor $T$ preserves $K$-indexed colimits separately in each variable (Example <a data-tag="070B" href="/tag/070B">9.7.3.4</a>). If $K$ is sifted, then diagonal map $\delta : K \rightarrow K \times K$ is right cofinal, so that the functor $T$ preserves $K$-indexed colimits (see Proposition <a data-tag="070C" href="/tag/070C">9.7.3.5</a>). Conversely, if $T$ preserves $K$-indexed colimits, then $\delta $ is right cofinal (Variant <a data-tag="05XP" href="/tag/05XP">9.1.5.4</a>), which implies that $K$ is sifted provided that it is nonempty (Proposition <a data-tag="02QM" href="/tag/02QM">9.7.2.8</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
